  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — plain browser script (loaded by [38;5;153mindex.html[39m via [38;5;153m<script src>[39m), holds [38;5;153mconst API_ENDPOINT = [39m
    [38;5;153m"https://api.example.com/login"[39m and a stub [38;5;153mlogin()[39m that never actually uses it yet.
  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — CommonJS Node modules, entirely separate from the browser page. No bundler, no test
    runner, no linter, no deps in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m.

  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m Not because the edit is big — moving one constant is trivial — but because "a new
  settings module" is structure this repo doesn't have, and the repo currently runs two incompatible module systems (a
  bare browser script and CommonJS). Where the settings module lives and how it's loaded determines what every future
  consumer has to do. So I'll work through questions → approaches → a design → a spec, rather than just making the edit.
  Say the word if you'd rather I keep it short.

  First question, and it's the one that shapes everything else.

  [1mThe fork:[0m "easier to change environments" can mean two very different things. Either the environment is chosen [3mat [0m
  [3mruntime[0m by the page itself (it looks at where it's running and picks), or it's chosen [3mat deploy time[0m by whoever ships
  the files (the right config is placed/swapped before the page ever loads). This isn't a naming difference — it decides
  whether the settings module contains all environments' URLs and selection logic, or just one environment's values.

  - [1mRuntime detection (hostname-based)[0m — one file ships everywhere; [38;5;153mlocalhost[39m → dev API, everything else → prod. Buys:
    zero deploy ceremony, and you can test env switching by just opening the page locally. Costs: every environment's
    URL is visible in the shipped source, and the mapping is a hardcoded list you must edit to add an environment. Rules
    out nothing permanently — cheap to replace later.
  - [1mRuntime override (global/query param)[0m — [38;5;153mwindow.__APP_CONFIG__[39m or [38;5;153m?env=staging[39m picks from a table. Buys: switch
    environments without a rebuild, useful for QA poking at staging from a prod build. Costs: same URL exposure, plus
    anyone can repoint your page at any environment — real consideration on a login form specifically.
  - [1mDeploy-time swap[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m holds one environment's values; CI or a deploy script drops the right variant in.
    Buys: shipped artifact contains only the environment it's for; no selection logic to get wrong. Costs: you can't
    switch by editing one line locally, and it presumes a build/deploy step this repo doesn't currently have.

  [1mMy recommendation: runtime detection by hostname.[0m This repo is a static page with no build step, and the login call is
  still a stub — introducing a deploy pipeline to solve a one-constant problem is backwards. Hostname detection gets
  you the "easier to change environments" benefit today, and if a real build step arrives later, collapsing the table
  down to a single injected value is a small, contained change.

